ng insertion\", \"Merge per-thread results sequentially preserving input order\".\n\nHypothesis: Using OpenMP parallel for schedule(static) on the group loop, each thread maintains its own local sorted list of k nearest neighbors (allocated once), and after the parallel region these lists are merged in order of increasing thread ID to preserve tie-breaking. This reduces synchronization overhead compared to critical sections while exploiting work sharing.\n\nWe'll set sources_kept to [] because integration_source_ids is empty.\n\nNow produce JSON with files: src/knn.c and src/knn.h (though knn.h may not need changes, but we must include omp.h? Actually, we can add #include <omp.h> in knn.c. No change needed for knn.h except maybe adding a constant MAX_K if we use it; better to avoid.\n\nLet's write the code.\n\nIn knn.c:\n\n#include \"knn.h\"\n#include <omp.h>\n\nchar knn(int n_groups, Group * groups, int k, Point to_evaluate) {\n    // Determine number of threads\n    int n_threads = omp_get_max_threads();\n    \n    // Allocate flat arrays for per-thread distances and labels\n    float *all_dists = (float *) malloc(n_threads * k * sizeof(float));\n    char *all_labels = (char *) malloc(n_threads * k * sizeof(char));\n    \n    // Initialize all to -1\n    for (int t = 0; t < n_threads; t++) {\n        float *dists_t = &all_dists[t * k];\n        char *labels_t = &all_labels[t * k];\n        for (int i = 0; i < k; i++) {\n            dists_t[i] = -1.0f;\n            labels_t[i] = -1;\n        }\n    }\n\n    #pragma omp parallel\n    {\n        int tid = omp_get_thread_num();\n        float *dists_local = &all_dists[tid * k];\n        char *labels_local = &all_labels[tid * k];\n\n        #pragma omp for schedule(static)\n        for (int i = 0; i < n_groups; i++) {\n            Group g = groups[i];\n            for (int j = 0; j < g.length; j++) {\n                float d = euclidean_distance_no_sqrt(to_evaluate, g.points[j]);\n                \n                // Insert into local top-k list\n                int x;\n                for (x = 0; x < k; x++) {\n                    if (d < dists_local[x] || dists_local[x] == -1.0f) {\n                        // Shift elements to make room\n                        for (int y = k - 1; y > x; y--) {\n                            dists_local[y] = dists_local[y - 1];\n                            labels_local[y] = labels_local[y - 1];\n                        }\n                        dists_local[x] = d;\n                        labels_local[x] = g.label;\n                        break;\n                    }\n                }\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    // Merge per-thread lists into global list (first thread's list as base)\n    float *global_dists = &all_dists[0];\n    char *global_labels = &all_labels[0];\n\n    for (int t = 1; t < n_threads; t++) {\n        float *dists_t = &all_dists[t * k];\n        char *labels_t = &all_labels[t * k];\n        \n        // For each entry in thread's list, try to insert into global\n        for (int idx = 0; idx < k; idx++) {\n            if (dists_t[idx] == -1.0f) break; // no more entries\n            \n            float d = dists_t[idx];\n            char lab = labels_t[idx];\n            \n            int x;\n            for (x = 0; x < k; x++) {\n                if (global_dists[x] == -1.0f || d < global_dists[x]) {\n                    // Insert here\n                    for (int y = k - 1; y > x; y--) {\n                        global_dists[y] = global_dists[y - 1];\n                        global_labels[y] = global_labels[y - 1];\n                    }\n                    global_dists[x] = d;\n                    global_labels[x] = lab;\n                    break;\n                } else if (d == global_dists[x]) {\n                    // Equal distance: skip because earlier thread's entry should remain\n                    break;\n                }\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    // Now compute most frequent label from global top-k list\n    qsort(global_labels, k, sizeof(char), compare_for_sort);\n\n    char most_frequent = global_labels[0];\n    int most_frequent_count = 1;\n    int current_frequency = 1;\n\n    for (int i = 1; i < k; i++) {\n        if (global_labels[i] != global_labels[i - 1]) {\n            if (current_frequency > most_frequent_count) {\n                most_frequent = global_labels[i - 1];\n                most_frequent_count = current_frequency;\n            }\n            current_frequency = 1;\n        } else {\n            current_frequency++;\n        }\n\n        if (i == k - 1 && current_frequency > most_frequent_count) {\n            most_frequent = global_labels[i - 1];\n            most_frequent_count = current_frequency;\n        }\n    }\n\n    free(all_dists);\n    free(all_labels);\n\n    return most_frequent;\n}\n\nBut note: The merge loop uses break on equal distance to skip insertion; that's correct because earlier thread already has that distance. However, we must ensure that if the global list is not full and there are entries with -1, we insert even if equal? Actually, if d == global_dists[x] and global_dists[x] != -1, then it's a duplicate of same distance; skip. If global_dists[x] is -1, then condition d < -1 is false (since -1 is sentinel), but we need to insert because slot empty. The loop checks if (global_dists[x] == -1.0f || d < global_dists[x]). So for empty slot, it will insert. That's fine.\n\nBut there is a subtlety: When merging, the order of insertion matters for tie-breaking when distances are equal across threads but not exactly same? Actually, if two entries have same distance from different threads, we skip later one; that matches sequential behavior because only first encountered (earlier group) should be kept. Since we process threads in increasing ID and each thread's groups are earlier than next thread's groups, this is correct.\n\nHowever, what about the case where within a single thread's list there could be multiple entries with same distance? No, insertion ensures uniqueness of distances because condition d < dists_local[x] prevents equal distances from being inserted. So each local list has strictly increasing distances (no duplicates). Good.\n\nNow, we need to ensure that the global list after merging also has no duplicate distances; our merge skips equals so it remains unique.\n\nPotential issue: The initial global list is thread 0's list. When merging later threads, if a distance from later thread is less than some existing entry but equal to another? Not possible because all distances are distinct within each list and across lists we skip equals.\n\nNow, correctness of tie-breaking for points with same distance that appear in different groups processed by the same thread: Since insertion uses strict less, only first occurrence per distance is kept. That's fine.\n\nThus this should produce correct output identical to sequential.\n\nWe also need to consider that k may be larger than number of distinct distances; but algorithm handles -1 sentinel.\n\nNow, we must ensure that the parallel for schedule(static) distributes groups contiguously in increasing order? With static scheduling and default chunk size (which is ceil(n_groups/n_threads)), each thread gets a contiguous block. That's what we rely on for tie-breaking ordering by thread ID. If we used dynamic, chunks would be assigned arbitrarily; then merging by thread ID order might not preserve global input order because groups from later threads could have earlier indices if they were processed out of order. So static scheduling is necessary to maintain the property that all points in lower-thread-ID chunk come before any point in higher-thread-ID chunk. Therefore we must use schedule(static) (or maybe guided with ordered? but simpler: static). We'll specify schedule(static).\n\nThus transformations include \"Use static scheduling to preserve group order across threads\".\n\nNow, write the final JSON.\n\nWe need to